---
  import DocLayout from '../../../components/DocLayout.astro';
  import { withBase } from '../../../lib/url';
  import { EXECUTABLE_NAME } from '../../../lib/releases';
---

<DocLayout
  title="Developer guide"
  description="The repository layout, backend modules, frontend, database, API surface, clients, and how to build and test."
>
  <p>
    This guide describes the repository for people who want to build, extend,
    or contribute to Sarthi Studio. The desktop app is Rust + SQLite with a
    hand-written static frontend; the Android client and VS Code extension
    are separate projects that talk to the desktop over a documented host
    API.
  </p>

  <h2>Repository layout</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>app/</code> — the Rust server and desktop binary (crate/binary <code>{EXECUTABLE_NAME}</code>).</li>
    <li><code>app/static/</code> — the app's UI: <code>index.html</code>, <code>app.js</code>, <code>styles.css</code>. No build step.</li>
    <li><code>docs/</code> — engineering documentation: architecture, feature status, testing, design system.</li>
    <li><code>android/</code> — React Native companion client and native emitters.</li>
    <li><code>vscode-extension/</code> — TypeScript extension with an embedded Node server.</li>
    <li><code>website/</code> — this site: Astro static site with MDX, sitemap, and Pagefind.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The backend modules</h2>
  <p>
    The server owns the router client, provider presets, the model catalog,
    the database, the workspace API, the agent run loop, the permission
    rules, the audit trail, and the keychain. Language servers provide
    diagnostics; the Debug Adapter Protocol talks to attach-style debuggers.
  </p>

  <h2>The frontend</h2>
  <p>
    A compact agent-style shell: thread sidebar, file workspace, tabbed
    editor with syntax highlighting, a real PTY terminal, Git operations,
    and a router-backed chat. The UI is static HTML/CSS/JS served by the Rust
    server; there is no build step in the app.
  </p>

  <h2>The database</h2>
  <p>
    SQLite at <code>~/.local/share/SAARTHI-STUDIO/studio.db</code> stores
    conversations, runs, steps, keys (with permissions), and usage. Studio
    refuses to run on a corrupt database; the recovery path rebuilds what it
    can. Back up the file before heavy operations.
  </p>

  <h2>The API surface</h2>
  <p>
    The loopback API is local-only; the opt-in Host API
    <code>/api/host/v1/*</code> is authenticated (bearer token) and TLS'd for
    the Android companion. The Host API is a documented subset of the
    desktop routes — no PTY, no process supervision. See
    <a href={withBase('/docs/api/')}>API reference</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Clients</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Desktop:</strong> native window (Tauri 2) or <code>--browser</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Android:</strong> direct router mode or Desktop Host mode.</li>
    <li><strong>VS Code:</strong> TypeScript extension with an embedded Node server.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Build, test, debug</h2>
  <p>
    Standard Rust workflow: <code>cargo build</code>, <code>cargo test</code>,
    and <code>cargo clippy</code>. The desktop <code>gui</code> feature adds
    the Tauri window. Frontend checks run with <code>npm run check</code> and
    the frontend suite inside the app.
  </p>

  <h2>Contributing</h2>
  <p>
    See the repository README, the feature status report, and
    <code>docs/TESTING-STRATEGY.md</code>. Website work uses the isolated
    <code>website/</code> toolchain with its own checks and commit lines.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
